THOUGHT: NullAway complains that the constructor doesn’t guarantee initialization of several fields marked (implicitly) as @NonNull: nsMap, nsMapGenerated, writer, strings, valuesParser, resourceIds. These are all assigned later in parse/decode, but from NullAway’s perspective they must be non-null after construction. The simplest fix is to initialize them to safe default non-null values in the constructor (or at declaration) so all control-flow paths, including the exception path, leave them non-null. I’ll inspect the rest of the file to see how these fields are used and then patch the constructor to assign defaults like new HashMap<>(), new HashSet<>(), EmptyCodeWriter, new String[0], new ValuesParser(new String[0], resNames), and an empty int[]; then run the NullAway build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/jadx && \
nl -ba jadx-core/src/main/java/jadx/core/xmlgen/BinaryXMLParser.java | sed -n '140,320p' && \
apply_edits() { ed -s "$1" <<'EOF'
65a
		nsMap = new HashMap<>();
		nsMapGenerated = new HashSet<>();
		writer = rootNode.makeCodeWriter();
		strings = new String[0];
		valuesParser = new ValuesParser(strings, resNames);
		resourceIds = new int[0];
.
EOF
} && apply_edits jadx-core/src/main/java/jadx/core/xmlgen/BinaryXMLParser.java && \
./gradlew jadx-core:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && \
grep -n "BinaryXMLParser" new_build_output.log || true && \
grep -n "NullAway" new_build_output.log | tail -n 5
```

